<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Bikaner State</h1><p class="st-meta">1465 CE – 1949 CE · capital Bikaner</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Rathore desert kingdom of northern Rajasthan, founded by a son of Rao Jodha, whose 20th-century Maharaja turned it green with the Ganga Canal.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1465 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-bika">Rao Bika</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-jodha">Rao Jodha</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jodhpur-state">Marwar</a>, led followers north into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jangladesh">Jangladesh</a> region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar</a> and carved out a kingdom from local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jat</a> and Bhati lands. He founded the city of Bikaner in 1488.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rai-singh-of-bikaner">Rai Singh</a> served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> as a general and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junagarh-fort">Junagarh</a> fort. Bikaner traded across the desert and was home to many Marwari merchant families. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganga-singh">Ganga Singh</a> (r. 1887–1943) raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganga-risala">Ganga Risala</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> corps, sat in the Imperial War Cabinet during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gang-canal">Ganga Canal</a>, opened in 1927 to irrigate the north of the state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bikaner signed a treaty with the British in 1818 and acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947. It merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-state-of-greater-rajasthan">Greater Rajasthan</a> in 1949 and is now a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bikaner-state.jpg" alt="Bikaner State" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bikaner.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1465 CE – 1949 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bikaner</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rao Bika, Rai Singh, Ganga Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bikaneri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bikaner</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1707&amp;focus=bikaner-state" aria-label="Bikaner State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6602 1151 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bikaner State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6922 1407L7018 1385M6922 1407L7364 1601M6922 1407L7364 1602M6922 1407L6996 1446"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6996 1446h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6922 1407h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6922 1407h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bikaner. <a href="/atlas?year=1707&amp;focus=bikaner-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
